Done. Added `store::rename` to `rust/src/store.rs:41-69`.

What it does:
- Reads the old file (`dir/<old_id>.note`); if it's missing or malformed, returns an error.
- Parses it with the same `splitn(4, SEP)` used by `load_all`, rewrites it under `dir/<new_id>.note` with `old_id`→`new_id` and `title`→`new_title`, preserving `pinned` (field 2) and `body` (field 3). Tags are recomputed on load from the unchanged body.
- Removes the old file (or rewrites in place when `old_id == new_id`).

Verified:
- `cargo build` and `cargo test` (existing 2 tests) pass.
- End-to-end run: after `rename`, the old `.note` file is gone, a new one exists, and `load_all` returns exactly one note with the new id/title while body, tags, and pinned state are unchanged (tested both with and without a trailing newline in the body).

One behavior note: `rename` mirrors `save`/`load_all` and does not trim the note body, so a file written with a trailing newline keeps it after renaming — consistent with the rest of the store, just not "cleaned".

